SETPRIV(1)                       User Commands                      SETPRIV(1)



NNAAMMEE
       setpriv - run a program with different Linux privilege settings

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       sseettpprriivv [options] _p_r_o_g_r_a_m [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Sets or queries various Linux privilege settings that are inherited
       across eexxeeccvvee(2).

       In comparison to ssuu(1) and rruunnuusseerr(1), sseettpprriivv neither uses PAM, nor
       does it prompt for a password. It is a simple, non-set-user-ID wrapper
       around eexxeeccvvee(2), and can be used to drop privileges in the same way as
       sseettuuiiddggiidd(8) from ddaaeemmoonnttoooollss, cchhppsstt(8) from rruunniitt, or similar tools
       shipped by other service managers.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ----cclleeaarr--ggrroouuppss
           Clear supplementary groups.

       --dd, ----dduummpp
           Dump the current privilege state. This option can be specified more
           than once to show extra, mostly useless, information. Incompatible
           with all other options.

       ----ggrroouuppss _g_r_o_u_p...
           Set supplementary groups. The argument is a comma-separated list of
           GIDs or names.

       ----iinnhh--ccaappss (++|--)_c_a_p..., ----aammbbiieenntt--ccaappss (++|--)_c_a_p..., ----bboouunnddiinngg--sseett
       (++|--)_c_a_p...
           Set the inheritable capabilities, ambient capabilities or the
           capability bounding set. See ccaappaabbiilliittiieess(7). The argument is a
           comma-separated list of ++_c_a_p and --_c_a_p entries, which add or remove
           an entry respectively. _c_a_p can either be a human-readable name as
           seen in ccaappaabbiilliittiieess(7) without the _c_a_p__ prefix or of the format
           ccaapp__NN, where _N is the internal capability index used by Linux. ++aallll
           and --aallll can be used to add or remove all caps.

           The set of capabilities starts out as the current inheritable set
           for ----iinnhh--ccaappss, the current ambient set for ----aammbbiieenntt--ccaappss and the
           current bounding set for ----bboouunnddiinngg--sseett.

           Note the following restrictions (detailed in ccaappaabbiilliittiieess(7))
           regarding modifications to these capability sets:

           •   A capability can be added to the inheritable set only if it is
               currently present in the bounding set.

           •   A capability can be added to the ambient set only if it is
               currently present in both the permitted and inheritable sets.

           •   Notwithstanding the syntax offered by sseettpprriivv, the kernel does
               not permit capabilities to be added to the bounding set.

       If you drop a capability from the bounding set without also dropping it
       from the inheritable set, you are likely to become confused. Do not do
       that.

       ----kkeeeepp--ggrroouuppss
           Preserve supplementary groups. Only useful in conjunction with
           ----rrggiidd, ----eeggiidd, or ----rreeggiidd.

       ----iinniitt--ggrroouuppss
           Initialize supplementary groups using initgroups3. Only useful in
           conjunction with ----rruuiidd or ----rreeuuiidd.

       ----lliisstt--ccaappss
           List all known capabilities. This option must be specified alone.

       ----nnoo--nneeww--pprriivvss
           Set the _n_o___n_e_w___p_r_i_v_s bit. With this bit set, eexxeeccvvee(2) will not
           grant new privileges. For example, the set-user-ID and set-group-ID
           bits as well as file capabilities will be disabled. (Executing
           binaries with these bits set will still work, but they will not
           gain privileges. Certain LSMs, especially AppArmor, may result in
           failures to execute certain programs.) This bit is inherited by
           child processes and cannot be unset. See pprrccttll(2) and
           _D_o_c_u_m_e_n_t_a_t_i_o_n_/_p_r_c_t_l_/_n_o___n_e_w___p_r_i_v_s_._t_x_t in the Linux kernel source.

           The _n_o___n_e_w___p_r_i_v_s bit is supported since Linux 3.5.

       ----rrggiidd _g_i_d, ----eeggiidd _g_i_d, ----rreeggiidd _g_i_d
           Set the real, effective, or both GIDs. The _g_i_d argument can be
           given as a textual group name.

           For safety, you must specify one of ----cclleeaarr--ggrroouuppss, ----ggrroouuppss,
           ----kkeeeepp--ggrroouuppss, or ----iinniitt--ggrroouuppss if you set any primary _g_i_d.

       ----rruuiidd _u_i_d, ----eeuuiidd _u_i_d, ----rreeuuiidd _u_i_d
           Set the real, effective, or both UIDs. The _u_i_d argument can be
           given as a textual login name.

           Setting a _u_i_d or _g_i_d does not change capabilities, although the
           exec call at the end might change capabilities. This means that, if
           you are root, you probably want to do something like:

           sseettpprriivv ----rreeuuiidd==11000000 ----rreeggiidd==11000000 ----iinnhh--ccaappss==--aallll

       ----sseeccuurreebbiittss (++|--)_s_e_c_u_r_e_b_i_t...
           Set or clear securebits. The argument is a comma-separated list.
           The valid securebits are _n_o_r_o_o_t, _n_o_r_o_o_t___l_o_c_k_e_d, _n_o___s_e_t_u_i_d___f_i_x_u_p,
           _n_o___s_e_t_u_i_d___f_i_x_u_p___l_o_c_k_e_d, and _k_e_e_p___c_a_p_s___l_o_c_k_e_d. _k_e_e_p___c_a_p_s is cleared
           by eexxeeccvvee(2) and is therefore not allowed.

       ----ppddeeaatthhssiigg kkeeeepp|cclleeaarr|<<ssiiggnnaall>>
           Keep, clear or set the parent death signal. Some LSMs, most notably
           SELinux and AppArmor, clear the signal when the process'
           credentials change. Using ----ppddeeaatthhssiigg kkeeeepp will restore the parent
           death signal after changing credentials to remedy that situation.

       ----sseelliinnuuxx--llaabbeell _l_a_b_e_l
           Request a particular SELinux transition (using a transition on
           exec, not dyntrans). This will fail and cause sseettpprriivv to abort if
           SELinux is not in use, and the transition may be ignored or cause
           eexxeeccvvee(2) to fail at SELinux’s whim. (In particular, this is
           unlikely to work in conjunction with _n_o___n_e_w___p_r_i_v_s.) This is similar
           to rruunnccoonn(1).

       ----aappppaarrmmoorr--pprrooffiillee _p_r_o_f_i_l_e
           Request a particular AppArmor profile (using a transition on exec).
           This will fail and cause sseettpprriivv to abort if AppArmor is not in
           use, and the transition may be ignored or cause eexxeeccvvee(2) to fail
           at AppArmor’s whim.

       ----rreesseett--eennvv
           Clears all the environment variables except TTEERRMM; initializes the
           environment variables HHOOMMEE, SSHHEELLLL, UUSSEERR, LLOOGGNNAAMMEE according to the
           user’s passwd entry; sets PPAATTHH to _/_u_s_r_/_l_o_c_a_l_/_b_i_n_:_/_b_i_n_:_/_u_s_r_/_b_i_n for
           a regular user and to
           _/_u_s_r_/_l_o_c_a_l_/_s_b_i_n_:_/_u_s_r_/_l_o_c_a_l_/_b_i_n_:_/_s_b_i_n_:_/_b_i_n_:_/_u_s_r_/_s_b_i_n_:_/_u_s_r_/_b_i_n for
           root.

           The environment variable PPAATTHH may be different on systems where
           _/_b_i_n and _/_s_b_i_n are merged into _/_u_s_r. The environment variable SSHHEELLLL
           defaults to //bbiinn//sshh if none is given in the user’s passwd entry.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

NNOOTTEESS
       If applying any specified option fails, _p_r_o_g_r_a_m will not be run and
       sseettpprriivv will return with exit status 127.

       Be careful with this tool — it may have unexpected security
       consequences. For example, setting _n_o___n_e_w___p_r_i_v_s and then execing a
       program that is SELinux-confined (as this tool would do) may prevent
       the SELinux restrictions from taking effect.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       If you’re looking for behavior similar to ssuu(1)/rruunnuusseerr(1), or ssuuddoo(8)
       (without the --gg option), try something like:

       sseettpprriivv ----rreeuuiidd==11000000 ----rreeggiidd==11000000 ----iinniitt--ggrroouuppss

       If you want to mimic daemontools' sseettuuiidd(8), try:

       sseettpprriivv ----rreeuuiidd==11000000 ----rreeggiidd==11000000 ----cclleeaarr--ggrroouuppss

